    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Denver & Rio Grande Railroad finished its Lake City Branch in 1889, cutting shipping costs and boosting mining activity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townoflakecityco.gov/an-introduction-to-lake-city-s-history"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Lake City sits four miles north of Lake San Cristobal in a valley where the Lake Fork of the Gunnison River meets Henson Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townoflakecityco.gov/an-introduction-to-lake-city-s-history"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Hinsdale County Historical Society, founded in 1973, preserves Lake City's mining‑era documents and photographs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_City%2C_Colorado"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Lake City’s name comes from nearby Lake San Cristobal, the second‑largest natural lake in Colorado.",
